Disney has been churning out family  favorite films for the better part of a  century now so there are a ton of great  animated and live-action films to choose  from in their collection it's becoming  increasingly difficult to pin down which  of their movies stand out as the most  important of all but here are our picks  for the 15 best disney movies not  including those blockbuster Marvel and  Star Wars adventures of course up what  makes this movie such an instant classic  is the charming partnership between its  main characters a young boy scout in a  stodgy old man who retrofits his house  to float up up and away to fulfill his  long denied dream of getting away from  it all with his late wife warm and  filled with colorful imagery and one gut  punch of emotional payoff up stands out  as an unforgettable animated adventure  and you'll never see helium balloons or  golden retrievers quite the same way my  name is Doug I have just met you and I  love you Wally the central characters in  wall-e may not say much but the movie  remains a showcase of what can happen  when top-notch animation meets unbridled  creativity part dystopian disaster and  part love story the film offers a  devastating look at the future of  mankind and earth if our wasteful ways  continue unchecked  Wally's cute robots only want to make  the world a better place though so  audiences are invited to remain  optimistic about the future without them  verbally telling us why Beauty and the  Beast the story of Belle Disney's most  academically inclined princess Beauty  and the Beast has claimed the hearts of  millions for good reason at its core  it's about a girl who decides not to  judge a book by its cover and finds her  own happy ending sure it's less than  ideal that she was imprisoned by the  Beast before falling in love with him  but the beauty of this Animated Classics  of visual dynamism overcomes those flaws  to make this movie a complete triumph  and the recent live-action adaptation  was almost as compelling as the original  frozen a celebration of both songs and  princesses frozen offers not one but two  imitable young ladies princesses Anna  and Elsa to fill the must have costume  racks while transforming Hans Christian  Andersen's The Snow Queen into something  far less dreary and much more whimsical  it's also stunning  beautiful with an icy landscape serving  as an elegant background to the thawing  sisterly relationship parents might be  more than ready to let the film  soundtrack go already but it's earworm  quality is just a small part of what  makes this a must watch for kids The  Incredibles the action-adventure flick  might have preceded Marvel's decade of  big-screen superhero domination but it  still holds up as a delightful reminder  of what makes costumed adventure stories  so fun to watch in the first place of  course The Incredibles is far more  cartoonish than anything in the MCU but  it takes full advantage of the quirky  possibilities of an animated superhero  movie with characters that boast a broad  range of abilities and it takes viewers  on a heartfelt family journey along the  way Cinderella Cinderella has become a  cornerstone of the Disney Catalog since  it's her castle that makes up Disney's  logo in all modern audiences might take  issue with the notion that Cinderella  only becomes the object of her Prince's  affection after getting a magical  makeover but Cinderella staying power is  this undeniable as a determination to  escape the clutches of Ramin steppe  monsters  inside out this Disney Pixar adventure  takes a clever look at the way the human  mind works animating the emotions and  impulses fighting for control of its  main character while trying to protect  the core memories making up the basis of  her personality oh sure we'll eat our  dinner right after you eat  in the story our young heroine Riley has  to adjust to a new life as her family  relocates to another city and that shift  is the spark that sensor from the  happy-go-lucky child she once was into  the rebellious tween phase a change that  happens to most people at some point  anyway so it feels very familiar and  authentically adolescent rather than  rely upon constructs of fiction like  most cartoons inside-out borrows from  reality to offer some heavy truths in an  amusing animated package Mary Poppins  author P L Travers was infamously  unhappy with Disney's adaptation of her  story but Mary Poppins arrived in  theaters as an instant musical evergreen  we can thank the movie's depiction of  its eponymous magical nanny for  memorable songs like a spoonful of sugar  and for raising the musical and visual  bar on the genre as a whole Aladdin  Disney really did take audiences to a  whole new world with this animated hit  Aladdin centered on the rags to riches  story of a street rat who stumbles upon  a magic lamp containing a ten thousand  year old genie who was all too happy to  join him on his magic carpet ride the  stunning scenery music and all the other  memorable characters are all beyond  reproach  but it's Robin Williams genie that  really elevates the picture to hilarious  new heights Peter Pan if there's one  thing we learned from Peter Pan it's to  never surrender that sense of youth and  Wonder and of course to never smile at a  crocodile there have been many terrible  attempts to recreate the magic of Peter  Pan and it's never Neverland but nothing  has come close to touching the simple  sophistication of the first version  which thrusts its sleepy little heroes  into a living dream that played with the  elements of fantasy like mermaids  fairies and sniveling old pirates the  film is fun plain and simple and mostly  stands the test of time because like its  title it never gets old Alice in  Wonderland the animated adaptation of  Lewis Carroll's story is a visual  extravaganza that plays well to children  and adults alike the movie follows its  title character down the rabbit hole  into a dreamlike and at times  nightmarish land of otherworldly  creatures that challenge her wits and  resolve the eerie landscapes were bested  only by its cast of memorable characters  movie is the definition of trippy and  still stands above the studios  live-action remake Finding Nemo finding  dory might have been more commercially  successful than its predecessor but  Finding Nemo set the tone for its  successful sequel in virtually every way  the film followed a clownfish named  Marlin and a forgetful blue tang named  Dory on their exhausting adventure to  find Marlins Lhasa and Nemo with an  actual ocean of obstacles while finding  Dory certainly touched on all the  successful elements of the first film  namely tracking down a missing fish  who's been scooped from the sea with the  help of some adorable Finny friends the  soul of the first adventure was so much  stronger Lady and the [ __ ] even  thinking about the spaghetti scene and  lady in the trap is bound to induce  feelings of both hunger and romance this  story of a proper society food shoe  accidentally lands herself in the  company of strays is a pure delight and  while Disney's 101 Dalmatians also had a  full-on litter of doggies to love  there's simply no film for a baby quite  as graceful as miss lady ma'am whose  friends are also as human as any  two-legged type could hope to be Toy  Story 3 this may have been the third  outing for woody buzz and the rest of  the gang but Toy Story 3 out shown the  previous two installments in the way of  emotional impact the film picks up with  Andy heading off to college we're having  a cowboy and space stall won't earn him  any cool points in his dorm the toys are  packed up for the Attic but are  accidentally sent to a daycare filled  with reckless children and a Bowie bear  together they have to fight their way  back to where they belong wherever that  might be now that Andy's no longer a kid  their journey offers an emotional  parable about the process of abandoning  childish things and really resonates  with audiences particularly adults who  might have been surprised to find  themselves bawling over the lost toys  that symbolized so many bittersweet  bygones The Lion King this family  favorite really does offer the full  circle of Disney's animated strengths  the film is rich with stunning  landscapes and teeming with character  variety and morals on top of all that  there are deeply identifiable sets of  emotion at play for kids it's easy to  understand Simba's joy desperation for  freedom and a sense of being lost  without his family framework for parents  protecting your kids at all costs  becomes the most base instinct of all so  everything about this movie story rings  true let an old pro show us done add in  one outstanding soundtrack and it's no  wonder the Lion King still reigns  supreme
 

